\textbf{Solution:} From the given problem, we have
\begin{align*}
S_1 &= \frac{1}{8}AB^2\pi = 3, \\
S_2 &= \frac{1}{8}BC^2\pi = 1, \\
S_3 &= \frac{1}{8}CD^2\pi = 7, \\
S_4 &= \frac{1}{8}AD^2\pi,
\end{align*}
Using the Pythagorean theorem, we can derive:
\begin{align*}
AC^2 &= BC^2 + AB^2, \\
AD^2 &= CD^2 - AC^2,
\end{align*}
Hence,
\begin{align*}
AD^2 &= CD^2 - BC^2 - AB^2, \\
\end{align*}
From the above equations, we reach:
\begin{align*}
AB^2 &= \frac{24}{\pi}, \\
BC^2 &= \frac{8}{\pi}, \\
CD^2 &= \frac{56}{\pi},
\end{align*}
Therefore,
\begin{align*}
AD^2 &= CD^2 - BC^2 - AB^2 = \frac{24}{\pi},
\end{align*}
Thus,
\begin{align*}
S_4 &= \frac{1}{8}AD^2\pi = \frac{1}{8} \times \frac{24}{\pi} \times \pi = \boxed{3}.
\end{align*}

